/// Phase B of plans/ITERATOR_REDESIGN.md — `ref(name) := expr;` local
/// binding form.
///
/// Parser-side coverage (this commit):
/// - `ref(name) := expr;` is recognized as a binding form.
/// - The bound variable carries `isRef: true` (same flag as
///   `ref(name) : T` parameter bindings), so the existing Phase B
///   closure-capture gate rejects captures of the binding too.
/// - `ref(name) := ...` is rejected with `::` or in a comptime-only
///   context (borrows are runtime constructs).
///
/// Deferred to Phase B.5 (codegen):
/// - Type-checking the RHS as a flowable / ref-yielding expression.
/// - The C-codegen plumbing so the binding's `T*` storage receives
///   the call's `T*` return value without an intermediate
///   type-mismatched temp. Today, end-to-end compile of a
///   `ref(name) := call(...)` to executable C still fails — only
///   the parser-side guarantees below are covered.
pragma(Pragma.AllowUnsafe);
// `ref(name) := expr;` with `::` is rejected — borrows are runtime.
comptime_expect_error(
  ref(r) :: i32(42)
);
// The closure-capture gate fires on a `ref`-bound local just as it
// does on a `ref`-bound parameter. The capture site here is the
// closure inside the `make_reader` function — `r` is declared as a
// ref-local via the binding form, then captured by the closure.
// Today this is rejected because the underlying gate triggers on
// `isRef: true`, which the binding form sets.
//
// NOTE: this test compiles the surface AST but the produced C will
// not link until the Phase B codegen story is fully wired (see
// plans/ITERATOR_REDESIGN.md). We rely on `comptime_expect_error`
// catching the closure-capture rejection during evaluation, which
// happens BEFORE codegen runs.
identity :: (fn(ref(p) : i32) -> ref(i32))(p);
comptime_expect_error(
  make_capturing_closure :: (fn(ref(p) : i32) -> Impl(Fn() -> i32))({
    ref(r) := identity(p);
    () => r
  })
);
// Smoke test: a function that does NOT bind via `ref(name) :=` still
// compiles cleanly. Confirms the binding-form changes don't false-
// positive on regular `:=` bindings.
test("regular := bindings still compile", {
  x := i32(5);
  y := x;
  assert(y == i32(5), "regular binding works");
});
